Held-out folio validation produced no structural violations among 3,930 types unseen during grammar development. This does not establish that the decomposition corresponds to linguistic morphemes. It establishes that a compact positional representation captures stable regularities across the manuscript transliteration.

The joint entropy of the four-slot token representation was 9.124 bits. The sum of marginal slot entropies was 10.306 bits, implying 1.182 bits of redundancy. Five conditioning axes—section, line position, previous suffix family, paragraph status, and quire—jointly explained 2.634 bits, or 28.87% of the token entropy. The largest incremental contribution after section and position came from the preceding suffix family. Approximately 71.1% remained unexplained by these production variables.

The residual was initially tempting to describe as lexical or semantic content. That interpretation was later withdrawn as an evidential claim. Residual entropy measures what the selected production model does not explain; it does not identify the cause of the residual.

### 4.2 Generator hierarchy

Twenty-three generators were evaluated using 84 metrics covering entropy, character and n-gram distributions, vocabulary growth, type-token behaviour, repetition, positional effects, autocorrelation, and structural constraints. Tolerances were derived from line-level bootstrap variation and cross-section variance. Split-half manuscript self-consistency produced a practical ceiling of 81/84 metrics.

The best fully generative model passed 59/84 metrics. Simpler character bigrams, copy-mutate processes, section-conditioned templates, slot grammars, and two-stream variants reproduced different subsets. A descriptive all-history avoidance model reached approximately 71/84 under cross-section validation but consumed the observed latent triple stream and was therefore not a free-standing generator. The hierarchy extended the literature's human-gibberish, self-citation, and table-and-grille controls by placing them alongside increasingly constrained slot and section models (Timm and Schinner 2020; Rugg and Taylor 2017; Gaskell and Bowern 2022).

Two lessons followed. First, a large collection of manuscript statistics can be reproduced without a semantic source message. Second, failure to reproduce the remaining metrics does not by itself favour cipher: it may indicate unmodelled production rules, transliteration effects, or ordinary corpus-specific variation.

### 4.3 Source-language resemblance

An early two-table hypothesis mapped one token class to function-word families and another to consonant-vowel rows. Pharmaceutical Latin ranked first in a sixteen-language consonant-distribution comparison, but Italian, Occitan, Portuguese, and Spanish occupied nearby positions. A leave-one-out analysis showed that one high-frequency assignment, *et*, dominated much of the bigram improvement. The model reproduced many surface metrics and cross-corpus correlations but did not recover a key, yield readings, or identify a source text.

This stage exposed the difference between **source compatibility** and **decipherment**. A distribution may resemble a corpus because of register, projection, or mapping choices without implying that the corpus language was encrypted. That recognition motivated explicit synthetic recovery tests.

---

## 5. Charged morpholocal calibration

### 5.1 Bounded cipher model

The first recoverability programme tested a mixed-unit homophonic and nomenclator proxy. Surface tokens mapped to a fixed latent inventory containing letter-, syllable-, preposition-, and whole-word-like units. The design varied:

- global versus partitioned keys;
- zero or low null inventories;
- balanced or unequal homophone classes;
- several source-register profiles;
- four homophone-selection policies;
- optional length-based surface selection.

The cipher arm paid for partition, mapping, null structure, policy, source model, selector, and data. Production controls included context-independent sampling, cell Markov models, copy-mutate processes, and permuted-cipher controls.

### 5.2 Formal v0.2 result

On 96 planted positives and 320 controls, the frozen v0.2 estimator recovered 34 positives and accepted no controls. Median mapping accuracy was 0.583, null F1 was 1.0, selector recovery was 1.0, and structural recovery was 0.573. The formal sensitivity gate failed.

The result was informative because it separated specificity from sensitivity. The model did not hallucinate payloads in the tested controls, but it could not recover the bounded cipher family reliably. Frequency-weighted and sticky line-reset homophone policies were especially difficult.

A literature review after this result showed that the implemented annealer was not a class-level test. Published systems often use higher-order language models, lexical constraints, optimised symbol assignment, specialised hill climbing, Bayesian sampling, lattices, or supervised synthetic training (Ravi and Knight 2011; Nuhn, Schamper, and Ney 2013, 2014; Dhavare, Low, and Stamp 2013; Chu, Valenti, and Knight 2020; Kambhatla, Mansouri Bigvand, and Sarkar 2018; Aldarrab and May 2021, 2022; Kambhatla, Born, and Sarkar 2023; Bruton, Beloucif, and Megyesi 2026). The v0.2 result was therefore registered as a decoder failure, not as rejection of historical nomenclators.

### 5.3 Decoder escalation and latent-order test

Subsequent versions added specialised search, beam decoding, Bayesian inference, policy-aware scoring, and neural models. A conditional randomisation statistic tested whether the recovered held-out latent sequence possessed more source-like transition order than within-line permutations.

On development data generated from related transition families, the test appeared promising. It detected 73 of 96 positives with six false positives among 64 controls. A generator-disjoint smoke test then replaced both sides. Positive trials used two independent Greek corpora and four independently coded homophonic renderers. Controls used the same 24-cell surface interface but latent sequences from an ordered HMM, motif grammar, topic finite-state process, or copied-and-mutated lines.

Performance reversed. Only 2/16 independent ciphers were detected, while 10/16 ordered non-message controls were accepted. The test had measured compatibility with the assumed external transition model, not the existence of an encoded source message.

### 5.4 Oracle source identifiability

A subsequent oracle experiment removed mapping inference and compared six source representations: a twelve-class projection, initials, character streams, syllable-like units, BPE units, and capped word identities. Source-family attribution reached 94–98% for most representations. Message sensitivity, however, was between 0% and 27.1% once thresholds constrained control false positives. No representation qualified for inferred mapping.

This established a second distinction:

> Source-family attribution is not message specificity.

A structured generator can produce sequences compatible with a source corpus without carrying an independently selected source message.

---

## 6. Multilingual recoverability frontier

### 6.1 Initial generic neural decoder

The v0.5 pilot crossed six languages, eight cipher families, three lengths, three noise levels, and four structured controls. The cipher families were monoalphabetic, homophonic, null homophonic, periodic polyalphabetic, feedback, nomenclator, substitution-plus-transposition, and fractionated systems. Training, development, and test corpus partitions were disjoint.

A compact Transformer was trained both with and without the true family label. It also attempted to classify whether the latent sequence was an independently selected source chunk or a generated control transformed by the same cipher.

The family-known arm achieved 9.03% message sensitivity, 5.16% control false positives, and 2.16% mean character recovery over all positives. The blind arm achieved 7.01% sensitivity and 1.67% recovery. The message-classification loss remained near \(\ln 2\), the value of an uninformative balanced classifier.

The conceptual defect was clear in retrospect. At the channel level, both positive and generated latent sequences were valid plaintext strings passed through the same cipher. The historical fact that one sequence was selected from a corpus and another emitted by a generator was not necessarily encoded in the ciphertext. The failure did not show that plaintext recovery was impossible. It showed that generic “messagehood” was an invalid target under those conditions.

### 6.2 Monoalphabetic positive result

The programme pivoted to family-specific solvers. A recurrence-canonicalised monoalphabetic solver used language-specific train-only character models and deterministic simulated annealing. Its locked test contained 360 unseen-key trials across six languages and three lengths.

Mean character recovery was 94.62%, compared with a 34.98% frequency-ranking baseline. Exact plaintext recovery was 29.72%. Every language exceeded 88%, and recovery at 192 and 384 characters exceeded 98%. At 96 characters, mean recovery was 87.40%, while exact recovery fell to 8.33%.

This positive result established that fresh-key, symbol-label-invariant recovery was feasible and that the corpus split and evaluation infrastructure could support strong results. It also showed that the generic Transformer’s failure was not a universal statement about synthetic cryptanalysis.

### 6.3 Fresh-key homophonic substitution

A six-language homophonic solver initially achieved 83.85% mean recovery over 360 trials. The aggregate concealed an English 384-character collapse to 9.23%, while German, Finnish, Turkish, and Arabic approached complete recovery. More text had driven the English search toward a high-scoring but incorrect basin.

The final homophonic bake-off compared classical restart curves, neural language-model beam search, recurrence Transformers, and neural-seeded classical refinement. The classical solver passed development only after 192 independent trajectories. On the untouched twenty-trial test it achieved 70.79% mean and 99.09% median recovery, but only 13/20 trials exceeded 70% (65.0%; Wilson 95% CI 43.3–81.9%), below the frozen 16/20 reliability requirement. This test was smaller than the 360-trial monoalphabetic benchmark because it was a computationally intensive, single-language, 384-character reliability confirmation after the broader six-language development programme; the resulting interval is correspondingly wide and limits class-level generalisation.

The distribution was bimodal. Thirteen trials were essentially solved; seven remained poor. Neural beam search remained near 24–26%. A million-example recurrence Transformer improved substantially, and a hybrid reached 87.99% mean recovery on development, but missed the median threshold. By contrast, a shared-code-pool control achieved 100% exact recovery on 20/20 trials.

The result distinguishes key reuse from fresh-key inference. A neural model can learn a stable code space while failing to generalise reliably to arbitrary unseen keys.

### 6.4 Nomenclators

Nomenclator experiments decomposed codeword recovery from residual character substitution. With the true character key supplied, code-symbol mapping accuracy was only 29.85% and occurrence-level word accuracy 44.36%, although expanded character recovery appeared high because only a few code symbols occurred in each 384-character sample. With true codewords supplied, residual substitution recovery was again bimodal: five trials approached complete recovery and three remained near 7–11%.

